WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.360
Here's another ribber cast on that is
stretchy, pretty, and easy to work.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.720
Similar to a single bed E-Wrap cast on,
we're using it for 1x1 ribbing.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.960
Now you need some skills
before you start this one.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.000
You need to have a basic 1x1 cast on of your choice.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.280
You'll be casting on with waste yarn.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.520
You need to know how to set your machine
for circular or tubular knitting.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.240
And understanding how to E-wrap helps.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.120
To make this cast on easier,
start with scrap and ravel.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.800
Cast on in 1x1 ribbing in any

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.600
method that you'd like,
and hang your ribbon cast on comb.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.680
Knit a few rows,

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.280
and then end your scrap
with a circular row of ravel cord.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.120
Knit across the main bed only

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.440
with the ravel cord, then knit across
the ribber only with ravel cord.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:03.040
Secure the ends of the ravel cord
and end with the carriage on the right.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.600
Change to your main yarn and e-wrap over

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.200
all needles, alternating between the main
bed needles and the ribber needles.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.000
Pulling the needles all the way out helps

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.760
prevent mistakenly wrapping
around the gate pegs.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:27.480
Wrap counterclockwise around the needles,
trying to keep the wraps somewhat loose.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.680
Thread up your carriage with your main

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.880
yarn, set your desired tension,
and knit across those E-wrapped stitches.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:41.440
That first row may be difficult to knit,
but scrapping on makes this easier.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.800
The cast on comb and weights are
in the scrap, not directly in the E-wraps.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:57.520
Continue knitting and mark the side
of the knitting that is facing you.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.680
Drop the work from the machine and examine

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.400
the right and wrong sides
of that cast-on edge.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.800
Notice that the side that was facing you

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.720
has small purl bumps, but the cast-on
edge that was away from you is neater.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.840
The difference isn't too noticeable,

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.040
but you might want to keep this in mind
when you're planning your garment.

